#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708570 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x \\sin{\\left(x z + y \\right)}$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. In the $x$ derivative, both the coordinate prefactor and the argument depend on $x$, so use both the product rule and the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\sin{\\left(x z + y \\right)}+x\\cdot \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(z\\right) \\\\ &=x z \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(x z + y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=x\\cdot \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=x \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=x\\cdot \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(x z \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(x \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x z + y \\right)}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708636 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: B to A; C_2: B to C; C_3: C to A](images/ec/ec44d8735e7e8ffb111f974314816d97f48c9b9f9f04e84d859ebb0da88b5d70.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. Which expression equals the following line integral?\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Trace the requested traversal. $C_1$ is followed. $C_2$ is reversed. $C_3$ is reversed. Reversal negates a vector line integral, and additivity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708649 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: B to A; C_2: C to B; C_3: A to C](images/35/35be973517dc028556a314ea1ca3a1cb9a461cc2c06c17d30b4a9697bef160e0.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. Which expression equals the following line integral?\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Trace the requested traversal. $C_1$ is reversed. $C_2$ is reversed. $C_3$ is reversed. Reversal negates a vector line integral, and additivity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{-C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=-\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708650 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: B to A; C_2: B to C; C_3: C to A](images/ec/ec44d8735e7e8ffb111f974314816d97f48c9b9f9f04e84d859ebb0da88b5d70.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. Which expression equals the following line integral?\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Trace the requested traversal. $C_1$ is reversed. $C_2$ is followed. $C_3$ is followed. Reversal negates a vector line integral, and additivity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{-C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=-\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708651 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. Which expression equals the following line integral?\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Trace the requested traversal. $C_1$ is followed. $C_2$ is reversed. $C_3$ is followed. Reversal negates a vector line integral, and additivity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708665 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: B to C; C_3: A to C](images/9d/9dd3f252121ae1d8b2c4df4a0297a243b57ea0b8fc891c915f75ce23f9af8801.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. Which expression equals the following line integral?\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "Trace the requested traversal. $C_1$ is reversed. $C_2$ is reversed. $C_3$ is followed. Reversal negates a vector line integral, and additivity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{-C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=-\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708672 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: D to C; C_2: B to C; C_3: D to A; C_4: B to A; C_5: A to C](images/54/5480c83b384f2abd235cdb9b380bd5a75f15a0de271692c288b5c93b340231da.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to D\\to C\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Trace both named loops in their given directions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_2-I_4-I_5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_1-I_3-I_5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested outer traversal has signed sum\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-I_1+I_2+I_3-I_4\n$$\n\nChoose the loop orientations so that their shared diagonal cancels and the outer segments have those signs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=(I_2-I_4-I_5)-(I_1-I_3-I_5)\\\\&=\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=(5)-(-3)\\\\&=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis uses orientation reversal and additivity; a closed path alone does not imply a zero integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708674 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: D to A; C_2: A to B; C_3: B to C; C_4: D to C; C_5: C to A](images/a1/a14ec792c3924723ca962f3921cd836bc3ddf012f4485bb447da2ca8cae10f15.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to D\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Trace both named loops in their given directions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_2+I_3+I_5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_1-I_4-I_5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested outer traversal has signed sum\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-I_1-I_2-I_3+I_4\n$$\n\nChoose the loop orientations so that their shared diagonal cancels and the outer segments have those signs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-(I_2+I_3+I_5)-(I_1-I_4-I_5)\\\\&=-\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=-(-3)-(-2)\\\\&=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis uses orientation reversal and additivity; a closed path alone does not imply a zero integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708679 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: A to D; C_3: C to D; C_4: B to C; C_5: A to C](images/f3/f336318d756a33f21dba0dba2bd52aaff23d8b266cec2c4f031bf7ef695dd080.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=4\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Trace both named loops in their given directions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-I_1-I_4+I_5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-I_2+I_3+I_5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested outer traversal has signed sum\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=I_1-I_2+I_3+I_4\n$$\n\nChoose the loop orientations so that their shared diagonal cancels and the outer segments have those signs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-(-I_1-I_4+I_5)+(-I_2+I_3+I_5)\\\\&=-\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=-(4)+(0)\\\\&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis uses orientation reversal and additivity; a closed path alone does not imply a zero integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708680 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: C to B; C_2: D to A; C_3: C to D; C_4: C to A; C_5: B to A](images/ca/ca44141368bac2cbba7ced4f762c7502e41fa94dff30ce4eebf4876603feb33e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-1\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Trace both named loops in their given directions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-I_1+I_4-I_5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_2+I_3-I_4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested outer traversal has signed sum\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-I_1+I_2+I_3-I_5\n$$\n\nChoose the loop orientations so that their shared diagonal cancels and the outer segments have those signs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=(-I_1+I_4-I_5)+(I_2+I_3-I_4)\\\\&=\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=(-1)+(-3)\\\\&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis uses orientation reversal and additivity; a closed path alone does not imply a zero integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708684 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: A to C](images/aa/aa0c4824cd83fc9db89a280bc804427adabf51610a1d62b39d1a7292ac16bb2e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_3$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Compare each arrow with the requested traversal. Reversed segments contribute negative integrals. By additivity,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_1-I_2-I_3\\\\6&=(6)-(2)-I_3\\\\6&=-I_3+4\\\\-I_3&=2\\\\I_3&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final value is in the requested arrow direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708685 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: B to C; C_3: C to A](images/38/38a4b8304ca0a8122168f6e01824a13e899daaeb9b1b71e5f0320323d5ee78c1.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Compare each arrow with the requested traversal. Reversed segments contribute negative integrals. By additivity,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_1+I_2+I_3\\\\0&=(0)+I_2+(-3)\\\\0&=I_2-3\\\\I_2&=3\\\\I_2&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final value is in the requested arrow direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708686 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_1$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Compare each arrow with the requested traversal. Reversed segments contribute negative integrals. By additivity,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-I_1+I_2-I_3\\\\6&=-I_1+(-3)-(-3)\\\\6&=-I_1+0\\\\-I_1&=6\\\\I_1&=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final value is in the requested arrow direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708694 :question/problem "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=14\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=4\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. Compare each arrow with the requested traversal. Reversed segments contribute negative integrals. By additivity,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_1-I_2+I_3\\\\14&=(4)-I_2+(5)\\\\14&=-I_2+9\\\\-I_2&=5\\\\I_2&=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final value is in the requested arrow direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708717 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left(- 3 x y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(- 4 y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(- 5 x z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(R\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=- 3 x y,\\quad Q=- 4 y z,\\quad R=- 5 x z\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(- 3 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 4 y z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(- 5 x z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708724 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 x z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(-3\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(R\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=4 y,\\quad Q=4 x z,\\quad R=-3\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(4 x z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(-3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708725 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left(5 x z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(- 4 x y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(- y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(R\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=5 x z,\\quad Q=- 4 x y,\\quad R=- y z\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(5 x z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 4 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(- y z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708740 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left(- x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(- z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(R\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=- x,\\quad Q=- z,\\quad R=4 y\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(- x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708742 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left(5 x y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(3 x z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in $\\mathbb{R}^{3}$. Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(R\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=5 x y,\\quad Q=4 y z,\\quad R=3 x z\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(5 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(4 y z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(3 x z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708764 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- x + 2 y$ at the points $(-2,-2)$, $(2,0)$, $(-1,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x + 2 y\\right)=-1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x + 2 y\\right)=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(-1\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,-2)&=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(2,0)&=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,-2)&=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/38/38fad205b37d770a5e5ea1761549f3736ffaf44e640fa1d6565cdb9dc33d7002.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708771 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=2 x - y$ at the points $(1,-2)$, $(0,-1)$, $(2,-1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x - y\\right)=2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x - y\\right)=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(2\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-1\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1,-2)&=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(0,-1)&=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-1)&=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/93/93096406c8aa72a211ef14419e35065076e4ed4dcc314e4b117c35496111964c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708781 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- 2 x - y$ at the points $(-2,-1)$, $(0,-2)$, $(0,2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 2 x - y\\right)=-2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 2 x - y\\right)=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(-2\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-1\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,-1)&=\\langle -2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(0,-2)&=\\langle -2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(0,2)&=\\langle -2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/2b/2b3d711b8443e4a71e9e94bbeadaa064a86c5242c471214e3afaf66424c8f569.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708785 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- 2 x + y$ at the points $(-1,0)$, $(0,0)$, $(-1,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 2 x + y\\right)=-2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 2 x + y\\right)=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(-2\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(1\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-1,0)&=\\langle -2,\\;1\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(0,0)&=\\langle -2,\\;1\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,-2)&=\\langle -2,\\;1\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/a6/a6ebf9d64011a1960745f8ca64d5c5796e5caf938609c64e916171bbc110bd4f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708787 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=2 x - y$ at the points $(1,-1)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x - y\\right)=2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x - y\\right)=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(2\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-1\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1,-1)&=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(2,0)&=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-2)&=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7851cfc1de8e1c3a8ad4ce66a2931d5fb4c05ef1f398bf88cd34cd316e4360.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708790 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\left(2 e^{y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left\\langle t,3 t^{2} - 1\\right\\rangle,\\quad 0\\leq t\\leq 1.\n$$ Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=2 e^{y},\\quad Q=4 e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe parametrization is not needed to express the integral using coordinate differentials.\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(2 e^{y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(4 e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708793 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\left(- 3 e^{y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand let $C$ be parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left\\langle t,t^{2} + 2\\right\\rangle,\\quad 0\\leq t\\leq 1.\n$$ Which coordinate-integral expression is equivalent to $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=- 3 e^{y},\\quad Q=4 e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe parametrization is not needed to express the integral using coordinate differentials.\n\nSubstituting into the identity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(- 3 e^{y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(4 e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708804 :question/problem "Match the coefficients of the coordinate differentials in\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(5 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(3 z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nwith the components of a vector field. Which field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$ gives this coordinate representation of $\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a piecewise-smooth path and a field defined on it,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_C \\left(P\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(Q\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(R\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\n$$\n\nHere the whole coefficients, including their signs, are\n\n$$\nP=2 y,\\quad Q=5 x^{2},\\quad R=3 z\n$$\n\nPlace those coefficients on the corresponding basis vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)&=\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(5 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(3 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}\\\\ \\int_C\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}&=\\int_C \\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(5 x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\left(3 z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708813 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{\\left(- x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)$ at the points $(2,-2)$, $(-1,-1)$, $(0,0)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(- x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot\\left(- x - y\\right)\\cdot(-1)=-\\left(- x - y\\right) \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(- x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot\\left(- x - y\\right)\\cdot(-1)=-\\left(- x - y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(-\\left(- x - y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-\\left(- x - y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFor substitution, write $t(x,y)=- x - y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt(2,-2)&=(-1)(2)+(-1)(-2)=0 \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-2)&=\\langle (-1)(0),\\;(-1)(0)\\rangle=\\langle 0,\\;0\\rangle \\\\ t(-1,-1)&=(-1)(-1)+(-1)(-1)=2 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,-1)&=\\langle (-1)(2),\\;(-1)(2)\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;-2\\rangle \\\\ t(0,0)&=(-1)(0)+(-1)(0)=0 \\\\ \\nabla f(0,0)&=\\langle (-1)(0),\\;(-1)(0)\\rangle=\\langle 0,\\;0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/b4/b4d231c7b3cc44fee29b4055d0e2851160c9b774b984e614a7d8c2e550999572.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708814 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=-\\left(\\frac{\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)$ at the points $(-1,1)$, $(-2,1)$, $(0,-1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(-\\left(\\frac{\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=-1\\cdot\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)\\cdot(-2)=2\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\left(\\frac{\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=-1\\cdot\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)\\cdot(-2)=2\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(2\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFor substitution, write $t(x,y)=- 2 x - 2 y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt(-1,1)&=(-2)(-1)+(-2)(1)=0 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,1)&=\\langle (2)(0),\\;(2)(0)\\rangle=\\langle 0,\\;0\\rangle \\\\ t(-2,1)&=(-2)(-2)+(-2)(1)=2 \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,1)&=\\langle (2)(2),\\;(2)(2)\\rangle=\\langle 4,\\;4\\rangle \\\\ t(0,-1)&=(-2)(0)+(-2)(-1)=2 \\\\ \\nabla f(0,-1)&=\\langle (2)(2),\\;(2)(2)\\rangle=\\langle 4,\\;4\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/d0/d01d806e662fbd4440b8a71755e0d7e438bf81c160889953e787f6799e7fcfe2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708834 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{\\left(x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)$ at the points $(2,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(-1,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot\\left(x - y\\right)\\cdot(1)=\\left(x - y\\right) \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot\\left(x - y\\right)\\cdot(-1)=-\\left(x - y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(\\left(x - y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(-\\left(x - y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFor substitution, write $t(x,y)=x - y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt(2,0)&=(1)(2)+(-1)(0)=2 \\\\ \\nabla f(2,0)&=\\langle (1)(2),\\;(-1)(2)\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-2\\rangle \\\\ t(0,1)&=(1)(0)+(-1)(1)=-1 \\\\ \\nabla f(0,1)&=\\langle (1)(-1),\\;(-1)(-1)\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;1\\rangle \\\\ t(-1,-2)&=(1)(-1)+(-1)(-2)=1 \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,-2)&=\\langle (1)(1),\\;(-1)(1)\\rangle=\\langle 1,\\;-1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/55/55944b4b37b8da336e75a22908ab096c9092922502261eb9e5ffd298f6e4cea3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708835 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=-\\left(\\frac{\\left(- x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)$ at the points $(0,1)$, $(-2,0)$, $(0,-1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(-\\left(\\frac{\\left(- x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=-1\\cdot\\left(- x - y\\right)\\cdot(-1)=\\left(- x - y\\right) \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\left(\\frac{\\left(- x - y\\right)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=-1\\cdot\\left(- x - y\\right)\\cdot(-1)=\\left(- x - y\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(\\left(- x - y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\left(- x - y\\right)\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFor substitution, write $t(x,y)=- x - y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt(0,1)&=(-1)(0)+(-1)(1)=-1 \\\\ \\nabla f(0,1)&=\\langle (1)(-1),\\;(1)(-1)\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ t(-2,0)&=(-1)(-2)+(-1)(0)=2 \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,0)&=\\langle (1)(2),\\;(1)(2)\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;2\\rangle \\\\ t(0,-1)&=(-1)(0)+(-1)(-1)=1 \\\\ \\nabla f(0,-1)&=\\langle (1)(1),\\;(1)(1)\\rangle=\\langle 1,\\;1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/14/149163a58cc8080096d38eab543f77c5b3e732de8b089843046a4f450eada599.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708843 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}$ at the points $(-2,-1)$, $(-1,1)$, $(0,1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)=- 2 x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)=y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,-1)&=\\langle (-2)(-2),\\;(1)(-1)\\rangle=\\langle 4,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,1)&=\\langle (-2)(-1),\\;(1)(1)\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(0,1)&=\\langle (-2)(0),\\;(1)(1)\\rangle=\\langle 0,\\;1\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/c6/c638ec05719029ee3b770cd52e09bf33cf3010eeeaf558160ca9ac1ae5d7130c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708848 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}$ at the points $(-1,-1)$, $(2,0)$, $(-2,2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)=x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)=y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-1,-1)&=\\langle (1)(-1),\\;(1)(-1)\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;-1\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(2,0)&=\\langle (1)(2),\\;(1)(0)\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;0\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,2)&=\\langle (1)(-2),\\;(1)(2)\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/c5/c585d66373e244ec9b8fbc574cf8d2b955fa07896574e751b76b5e12cc63c03e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708852 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - y^{2}$ at the points $(-1,2)$, $(1,0)$, $(0,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - y^{2}\\right)=x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - y^{2}\\right)=- 2 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-1,2)&=\\langle (1)(-1),\\;(-2)(2)\\rangle=\\langle -1,\\;-4\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(1,0)&=\\langle (1)(1),\\;(-2)(0)\\rangle=\\langle 1,\\;0\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(0,-2)&=\\langle (1)(0),\\;(-2)(-2)\\rangle=\\langle 0,\\;4\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/9b/9b6e0ed6a6fe35c9710580b04ed523f1b79fa556346ed41c2ea7435068ec4633.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708856 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + y^{2}$ at the points $(-1,-2)$, $(2,-1)$, $(1,0)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction. A blue ring around a dot denotes a zero vector." :question/worked-solution "The gradient is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. Take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)=- 2 x \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)=2 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-1,-2)&=\\langle (-2)(-1),\\;(2)(-2)\\rangle=\\langle 2,\\;-4\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-1)&=\\langle (-2)(2),\\;(2)(-1)\\rangle=\\langle -4,\\;-2\\rangle \\\\ \\nabla f(1,0)&=\\langle (-2)(1),\\;(2)(0)\\rangle=\\langle -2,\\;0\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the first component as horizontal displacement and the second as vertical displacement. Place each tail at its stated point and compare both direction and length. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/cf/cfcd62448cbd1f94e456d857967313c7a05425e34b9f563f4c16ecf412a3b3f0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708868 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf i-x\\,\\mathbf j$ along the closed curve $C$ traversed once according to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation is the work done by $\\mathbf F$ around the entire closed curve:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong $C$, we have $x=3\\cos t$ and $y=2\\sin t$. Substituting these coordinates into the field and differentiating the path gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i-3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=-3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product, keeping the signs of both components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\n&=(2\\sin t)(-3\\sin t)+(-3\\cos t)(2\\cos t)\\\\\n&=-6\\sin^2t-6\\cos^2t=-6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\int_0^{2\\pi}-6\\,\\mathrm dt\n=\\left[-6t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}=-12\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe parametrization moves counterclockwise, while the field opposes that motion, consistent with the negative result."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708869 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x-y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the ellipse $x^2+4y^2=4$, traversed once clockwise. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\Gamma=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{2^2}+\\frac{y^2}{1^2}=1.\n$$\n\nTo traverse it clockwise, use\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i-\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $(2,0)$ the velocity is $(0,-1)$, so this parametrization starts downward and has the required orientation. Along the path, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=-\\sin t$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2\\cos t+\\sin t)\\,\\mathbf i+(2\\cos t-\\sin t)\\,\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=-2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i-\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product contains a term that varies along the ellipse:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\n&=-2\\sin t(2\\cos t+\\sin t)\\\\\n&\\quad-\\cos t(2\\cos t-\\sin t)\\\\\n&=-4\\sin t\\cos t-2\\sin^2t\\\\\n&\\quad-2\\cos^2t+\\sin t\\cos t\\\\\n&=-2-3\\sin t\\cos t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate over one complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(-2-3\\sin t\\cos t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[-2t-\\frac32\\sin^2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=-4\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe varying term integrates to zero over the closed path; it cannot simply be discarded pointwise. The negative sign agrees with clockwise traversal against the rotational part of the field."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708871 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=-y\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j$ along the ellipse\n\n$$\n4x^2+y^2=16,\n$$\n\ntraversed once counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Write the ellipse in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{2^2}+\\frac{y^2}{4^2}=1.\n$$\n\nIts horizontal and vertical semiaxes are $2$ and $4$. A parametrization that traces it once counterclockwise is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i+4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ the path starts at $(2,0)$ and moves upward, confirming the orientation. The circulation is\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the ellipse, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=4\\sin t$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=-4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=-2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf i+4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\n&=(-4\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)+(2\\cos t)(4\\cos t)\\\\\n&=8\\sin^2t+8\\cos^2t=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\int_0^{2\\pi}8\\,\\mathrm dt\n=\\left[8t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}=16\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708885 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(3,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWith $\\mathbf a=(1,1)$ and $\\mathbf b=(3,2)$, a parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=(1+2t,1+t),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n\nIts endpoints are $\\mathbf r(0)=(1,1)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(3,2)$. Computing the derivative and speed gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=(2,1),\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt5.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates and expand their product:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(1+2t)(1+t)=1+3t+2t^2.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\sqrt5\\int_0^1(1+3t+2t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left[t+\\frac32t^2+\\frac23t^3\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left(1+\\frac32+\\frac23\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{19\\sqrt5}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708898 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_C (2x+1)e^{xy}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor $\\mathbf a=(0,1)$ and $\\mathbf b=(1,2)$, take\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=(t,1+t),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n\nThe endpoint check gives $\\mathbf r(0)=(0,1)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(1,2)$. Also,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=(1,1),\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{1^2+1^2}=\\sqrt2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting both coordinates gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(2t+1)e^{t(1+t)}=(2t+1)e^{t+t^2}.\n$$\n\nThus the required integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(2t+1)e^{t+t^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of the exponent is $1+2t$, which matches the factor outside the exponential. Set $u=t+t^2$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=(1+2t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The bounds change from $t=0,1$ to $u=0,2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(2x+1)e^{xy}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\sqrt2\\int_0^2e^u\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\sqrt2[e^u]_0^2\\\\\n&=\\sqrt2(e^2-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
